   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.4. Strengthened social dialogue and labour relations laws, processes and institutions</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. Enterprise-level workplace cooperation enhanced through the establishment of bipartite COVID-19 task forces, to facilitate a structured return to work in the Godrej &amp; Boyce Mfg. Co. Ltd enterprises in 2021.

2. As a basis for effective workplace information sharing, good practices undertaken by companies in responding to the COVID-19 pandemic have been documented by the Employers&apos; Federation of India (EFI) and the All India Organisation of Employers (AIOE). They have shared these good practices amongst their member companies. This report illustrates the value of good labour relations practices in effectively responding to the crisis and supported other companies to improve their processes. 1. Guidelines to establish enterprise-level COVID-19 task forces were developed in 2020 by the ILO to facilitate a structured return to work through the joint effort of employer and workers/unions. To implement the guidelines, the ITC-Turin Centre developed and delivered training to a bipartite group of trainers. In total, 30 trainers were trained (10 women), from employers&apos; organisations (All India Organisation of Employers, Employers Federation of India and Confederation of Indian Industries) and trade unions (Self-Employed Women&apos;s Association, All India United Trade Union Centre, Hind Mazdoor Sabha, Bharatiya Mazdoor Sangh, Indian National Trade Union Congress, Labour Progressive Federation, United Trade Union Congress, National Front of Indian Trade Unions and All India Trade Union Congress). The participants included 6 trainers from Godrej group of companies. 

2. The ILO developed the &quot;Towards resilient social dialogue in South Asia&quot; report (published in October 2020). This report provided constituents with easily accessible information on bipartite social dialogue mechanisms, namely collective bargaining and workplace cooperation pertaining to India. It also provided the basis for targeted activities in the current biennium, to strengthen social dialogue in India. The report was disseminated via the ILO website and Country Offices.

3. The ILO supported Employers&apos; Federation of India (EFI) and the All India Organisation of Employers (AIOE) to undertake the survey with more than 20 enterprises in India. In this regard, the ILO developed and workshopped a questionnaire and enterprise selection criteria. The ILO also developed a composite report, based on the reports of EFI and AIOE.</narrative>
